            <ArticleTitle>Ecthyma gangrenosum caused by Citrobacter freundii.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-220996</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-220996</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 55-year-old man undergoing chemotherapy for recurrent multiple myeloma presented with a 2-day history of bilateral lower leg rash with pain and oedema. On examination, there were numerous non-palpable retiform pruritic patches over both lower legs. Skin pnch biopsy demonstrated a diffuse interstitial neutrophilic infiltrate with necrosis. Peripheral blood and skin tissue cultures both isolated Citrobacterfreundii, consistent with a rare form of ecthyma gangrenosum. The patient responded with appropriate antibiotic therapy and removal of medical port. He made a full recovery from this infectious complication of his underlying immunosuppression.</AbstractText>
